#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/id #uuid "1c03777d-2ead-5333-bf75-0e9a96fdb0e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9563" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2 , \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2),$ and $(-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/aa822f0b9259d6d9eb5c6f439588e70d4b9bee16b2b868fae36c7cbac72d3c3f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = -3xy^2$ and $Q(x,y) = y+\\cos x .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)[x]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)(y-(-y))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2y\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-6{y}^{3}+2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-6\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{2}+{y}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-24+4)-0 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/id #uuid "a9d748d6-d688-5b77-a250-cb534a371765" :question/math-academy-id "e-9564" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3),$ and $(1,0),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (- 2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/02964b07e45daba2d560fa16d34bcc92ddb8d0e0e83f644f8284dbe9d4b4557b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) =  7x^2y^6 +x^3$ and $Q(x,y) =- 2xy^7+4x^2y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2})+(-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}7{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/id #uuid "ed1dfb37-d01e-5d35-82f2-a441cdd54f3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9565" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x , \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x$ and $Q(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({2}^{2}-2)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,dy \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8ba9128-17dd-54d7-abc8-d292906d45b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168632} {:answer/id #uuid "ff00d4ca-0557-57cd-a01a-aa8a298ad0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168633} {:answer/id #uuid "398d552a-b5ab-5d0e-994a-2c5e945b88ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168634} {:answer/id #uuid "4392582e-f5b3-5716-b120-796cb8c58f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168635} {:answer/id #uuid "79803fbb-50fa-57d9-a270-e130b7d932e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff00d4ca-0557-57cd-a01a-aa8a298ad0a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168633} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a46733a-cc9a-5e70-9bb6-b8c93a8badfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4e547a4-3c18-52fb-b4b9-408da110d4a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126820" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}8xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}y\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(2)^{2}y-4(-1)^{2}y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12y\\,dy \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "958de1d6-be32-553d-8d66-2d59c0ddd0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{-1}^{2}x(2y - 5)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168650} {:answer/id #uuid "993eec8c-adb1-5f3b-a5a4-9bf8186d8107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{2}(x^{2} + 5y)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168651} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7b9296-4fd2-5fcd-98c6-37a497b7f578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{2}(x^{2} - 5y)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168652} {:answer/id #uuid "c72058f2-c38d-5413-a9be-48c7bb164a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{2}x(2y + 5)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168653} {:answer/id #uuid "46e22be8-54a1-527b-a8ee-a4d73f417264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{-1}^{2}x(2y + 5)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c72058f2-c38d-5413-a9be-48c7bb164a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{2}x(2y + 5)dydx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168653} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d3a1312-b415-507d-afe0-200629efab71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bab01488-68e9-5dde-9b92-c15b0288a3e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127072" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+5xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a19fa5fa-140d-5083-8836-3dbc68de1c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343093} {:answer/id #uuid "1d737684-4a8f-55f8-bf66-0c9e44a3ab9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343094} {:answer/id #uuid "4af707bd-ffbc-55e5-85e2-5b5b455b5b93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343095} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3bfa35-e4e1-5d73-a360-a9be7aac9fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343096} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c90b41-e93a-51cb-af22-01b32345b4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a19fa5fa-140d-5083-8836-3dbc68de1c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343093} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e9e5248-5dcd-5e65-b949-370ed092c98c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b027c7d9-c33a-5bd1-a380-50294001a9fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-127206" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨y+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and  $(0,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/98d7da0c7cd1e1f1006c1db74ae1a1e1f198d9066df6585745ee36548f0b8d96.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2xy.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aaab870-fee8-5778-9e62-63cbd726d6ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343100} {:answer/id #uuid "6104b009-0a46-52a6-91bf-52bf3ba3c16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343101} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff19b65-7c6f-5877-a33c-1f3b1fb0aaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343102} {:answer/id #uuid "1db5158d-6b9e-5407-98f3-e4ca4ce31ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+12y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343103} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2c4738-5105-58fd-803c-46b791e06c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff19b65-7c6f-5877-a33c-1f3b1fb0aaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343102} :answer-field/id #uuid "967463aa-9fb3-5356-9566-5d49cd143f5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b4bd86f-3707-5569-a669-abddd19a5594" :question/math-academy-id "q-127228" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(3,0),(3,2),$ and $(0,2),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+12xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/23066b23aebf332921c22a0439a414ade8dffd2a270641d186da0ed0e9488821.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=12xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x{y}^{2}+12x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e002c432-6b41-58d9-988e-71a456e9e481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343107} {:answer/id #uuid "454fa13f-8d25-5391-b9d6-f33c1dbbf2a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{y}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343108} {:answer/id #uuid "de344b95-05d4-587a-9ef5-2ce0bbf5de87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343109} {:answer/id #uuid "c02a027a-6200-595f-baf9-dafbc25357a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{y}^{2}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343110} {:answer/id #uuid "2112195a-76e2-599e-aaa8-e3b6efea8bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de344b95-05d4-587a-9ef5-2ce0bbf5de87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343109} :answer-field/id #uuid "4206a986-c910-5991-9286-4b258d96d2d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5093f0a0-5066-5cea-9d9a-27301fc3151a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127229" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}\\,i+6x{y}^{4}\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =24x{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{3}+24x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cb8a4e1-3751-561c-8686-4a2d4f31fef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343114} {:answer/id #uuid "14fe9f41-9ece-51d5-af87-bca1bd7ad82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}(x-5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343115} {:answer/id #uuid "fb03450b-8490-5f1a-ad03-ea0ea97ba6d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}{\\int }_{-3}^{1}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343116} {:answer/id #uuid "ead4e0e5-f34e-572a-99e7-48f11f93eefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}(2xy+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343117} {:answer/id #uuid "28d66978-b7f8-53ff-8491-0615455b1899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}{\\int }_{-3}^{1}(5{x}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cb8a4e1-3751-561c-8686-4a2d4f31fef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343114} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed46141b-e20e-540b-9ec5-0fa9b3f0c51f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f5a027b-f99f-544f-9b6d-7009a16f6fd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-127231" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-3,-1),(-1,-1),(-1,1),$ and $(-3,1),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x)\\,i+(-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4})\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/5497f23e51d1e745fef1cf39d6c1a8ada22b25e8c9c7b87f5d5338e85a941739.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2yx+{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2yx+4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2yx+{y}^{3})+(-2yx+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "781e8ea0-cccd-5ae4-91c0-e4088a63b289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343121} {:answer/id #uuid "d967f26b-a825-5d15-9bfc-25c118cd8a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343122} {:answer/id #uuid "8abd0ed1-ccd3-53c4-9e86-17d9a2972922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343123} {:answer/id #uuid "b1098e51-63e5-537f-9005-51b9afd0ee5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343124} {:answer/id #uuid "44ba855d-8d3f-5871-807b-b1a0d735782b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8abd0ed1-ccd3-53c4-9e86-17d9a2972922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343123} :answer-field/id #uuid "c746d89b-0283-592e-98d3-4260895b3921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3590b805-95c9-538c-8659-4facc50b914a" :question/math-academy-id "q-127303" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x,\\,{x}^{5}-3y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/35e378bca35f4b0822c713556065415d59b5c566ec8fcc8e3124256aac7b7f14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{5}-3y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-(-1))\\,dy \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee01bec-10a6-56d0-a6a6-0a17b71d45ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343128} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2118a5-ce28-5d05-b475-86c3270e9784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343129} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2cc6c8-464a-59a5-96e8-656aabba7e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343130} {:answer/id #uuid "412008c2-86a1-55f2-b0d2-45e58d215b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343131} {:answer/id #uuid "0f89ea17-8866-5193-adc9-4d04816a65a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f89ea17-8866-5193-adc9-4d04816a65a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343132} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1dfdd52-f2b0-574b-bf39-618cb72cfe47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7259232a-99ab-558a-be32-b0525f58c89e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127308" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3},\\,x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/2696cdadd3cb020cfbe343b9c372893ac18b63f3548ff71003b7328b19de39d5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-3{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(-3(1)^{3}-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-(-3(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}{y}^{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6)\\,dy \\\\  & =-6y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef6acfa9-5471-59a3-8a05-5f7f2e541e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343135} {:answer/id #uuid "623db9e8-56f8-525b-bb6e-41e1865083e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343136} {:answer/id #uuid "88445497-b1ab-50b3-a14a-9825368bfead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343137} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4910d2-2735-5005-b68c-c8e5bb6815b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{50}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343138} {:answer/id #uuid "4f824283-0d18-5dd2-9a5b-812865130533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef6acfa9-5471-59a3-8a05-5f7f2e541e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343135} :answer-field/id #uuid "0425be1a-7849-5c06-aafe-8f78d3552294" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa992dc9-ced4-5b8b-9b30-3340dc9cd139" :question/math-academy-id "q-127309" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y},\\,3{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/f6e80e3010ec7c40d3f59dbb1da4e77c985c889a720b6f954177d8ad83a01351.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12yx,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}x+3{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}+3x)\\,dx \\\\  & =(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =22-0 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb70e29b-8e55-55e0-89c3-8f1a00af0585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343142} {:answer/id #uuid "5afee28c-5803-5da5-abe7-f7e5a8714186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343143} {:answer/id #uuid "43fc078d-02de-5571-a12a-4a1aee55bab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343144} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c141e-27fb-5065-b8b9-bf744c309426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343145} {:answer/id #uuid "8b755a5c-4951-53e6-a617-9e102bf53ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5afee28c-5803-5da5-abe7-f7e5a8714186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343143} :answer-field/id #uuid "5081af50-ef6c-59bc-8282-e57c5cedbb11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98a60dee-772a-58ff-8285-32051d418b46" :question/math-academy-id "q-127316" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x-\\sqrt{y},\\,y-\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/c4dd4d03bdfcf6c3d597e2fdef8681e4f3316b3f276cb9967933b61b0dc90976.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x-\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y-\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2076d564-0556-5136-b574-60c508f11f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343149} {:answer/id #uuid "dc11b0d1-13cf-5819-9ed4-52331223af5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343150} {:answer/id #uuid "b0449da3-dfbb-594e-b857-3c2dec7d11b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343151} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7fa986-6118-5b32-93f2-3612ae6aa923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343152} {:answer/id #uuid "2e07e744-b913-5b62-b418-7434da6a9458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e07e744-b913-5b62-b418-7434da6a9458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343153} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6beec10-0cdd-5863-92f4-ff35440d7842" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8d9cccd-74f9-5ccf-9477-b13151692d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-127322" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\sqrt[3]{y}+x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/ea6c25e40b6683d9ff4a4884dbbda708fd79dbd79d0b50730f9ad6e165d2eb58.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}+x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2{x}^{3}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-2(2)^{3}y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-16y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[2y-8{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)-8(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a346aef0-72a7-556b-9da9-dc2e09bb9b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343156} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e81694-f729-52cb-a94c-d459c3785d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343157} {:answer/id #uuid "a39763a2-d6dd-5e5f-ae2e-d502a6ba0feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343158} {:answer/id #uuid "5286fdcf-bddf-5afa-b749-7940367caa42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343159} {:answer/id #uuid "0a66779e-1bc0-5c79-b222-3fc19b38e171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a39763a2-d6dd-5e5f-ae2e-d502a6ba0feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343158} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3e63c99-0f7f-53c5-b508-19066832e336" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "457c1d40-2c35-56c1-954e-e78212d935bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-127323" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}+2y,\\,-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,1),$ and $(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/3ad0a876fe801980a2c3724a639fc6c379db750635c0278a10a5094e2d64c34a.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-2y\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2y}^{2y}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}-10x]_{-2y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{y}^{2}-20y)-(24{y}^{2}+20y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-40y)\\,dy \\\\  & =-40{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-40(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b53d5554-5b82-55ea-ac3d-b3defc6a3e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343163} {:answer/id #uuid "67d20595-783e-54a3-922b-81dfa8eca608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343164} {:answer/id #uuid "34799e19-7d25-5096-b4d8-1f4abc550d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343165} {:answer/id #uuid "82a5a9ab-c6e3-575b-8e5a-82fcee8f8c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343166} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7fa4cc-ee62-5443-a78e-4f7cded22bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7fa4cc-ee62-5443-a78e-4f7cded22bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343167} :answer-field/id #uuid "51e161f6-6faa-5c07-8b7b-bbca0f2915cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b92140a8-c7a1-55ab-87e2-787946a3ce82" :question/math-academy-id "q-127324" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b7557ca-c141-5c40-81a6-fe9045290dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343170} {:answer/id #uuid "67ecb9eb-9bf2-5665-a74d-d2a62efb4bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343171} {:answer/id #uuid "1094ca62-c7bc-5cf8-9ae6-200e724b585d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343172} {:answer/id #uuid "1b74332f-f130-5c5c-8694-d43303ba0ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343173} {:answer/id #uuid "31332299-c1b3-5389-8f8c-f3500eea8d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b7557ca-c141-5c40-81a6-fe9045290dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343170} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a5d9e35-5943-5b72-8aa1-af99299b84c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f5c8c07d-635c-549f-b083-326a23c6263e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127325" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,{x}^{3}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/006d80a7dbe4d03b78cc5fb1a642405e3fdfdd69ec9d80014ee8e0355ad1ae73.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y/2}^{y/2}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[x]_{-y/2}^{y/2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[\\frac{y}{2}-(-\\frac{y}{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a6b5fbf-a350-5309-9b03-98787cd3081a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343177} {:answer/id #uuid "15e8270f-cc2b-5ffc-919b-936783a7b749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343178} {:answer/id #uuid "b0405d3c-0972-5690-9373-c29c85091718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{55}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343179} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc017d6-5536-5d2a-b376-20fd77ab6153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343180} {:answer/id #uuid "e52f70a9-241f-583d-88b5-0cf2702d746a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e8270f-cc2b-5ffc-919b-936783a7b749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343178} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec2b585-e2e0-51ce-bae9-2d282fca782d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b8359eb-af00-56dd-8f05-0df9950458a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127365" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨30xy,\\,15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y,$ $\\,x=-\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/24369439bc62cf06084a43d97dff0cee4e4089fd103511ef73d9dd5af1256ebb.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=30xy$ and $Q(x,y)=15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=30y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =30y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(30y+30y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}60y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y[x]_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y+\\sqrt{y})\\,dy \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+{y}^{3/2})\\,dy \\\\  & =60{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{2{y}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =60(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{5})-0 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
